1 NOTE Married 1630 Marie-Marguerite LeGardeur at Thury-Harcourt, Normandie, France; sources: family (3 children) is listed in Jette pg 713-714;  Drouin pg 834; <http://www.genealogie.com/cgi-bin/fo/afficher.cgi?i> (copy filed 29-12).
2 CONT A monument at Quebec City to LOUIS HEBERT has a plaque on the backside listing JACQUES LE NEUF and his spouse, MARGUERITE LEGARDEUR, among others, as settlers of Quebec City in 1636.  This info gathered from the internet and is fi
2 CONC led L-16-10.
2 CONT Database of Gilles C. Paquette from the internet (filed 29-13).
2 CONT 1666 Census of Trois-Rivieres (the first census taken in Canada) shows Jacques Leneuf, age 60, living with his brother, Michel, age 65.  Jacques' family is listed as follows: wife, Marguerite LeGardeur, age 58. also living with th
2 CONC em are the following domestics: Pierre Millet,71; Pierre Lolat, 23; Laurens Drout, 24; Vincent Marty, 27; Jean Jacques Bourgeois, 17; Jean Bellamy, 18; Estienne Bocart, 25.  Source is filed under censuses.
2 CONT <http://www.genealogie.com/cgi-bin/fo/afficher.cgi?i> (copy filed 29-12) shows the following sources: Jette pg 713; "Memoires de la Societe Genealogique Canadienne Francaise" Vol 51 pg 215; "Dictionnaire Genealogique de nos Origin
2 CONC es"--Denis Beauregard Vol 1 pg 68; "Dictionnaire Biographique du Canada" Vol 1 pg 478.
2 CONT <http://www.sylvestre.qc.ca/0000341.htm> contains information about Mathieu Leneuf and his family and can be found in Mathieu Leneuf's [#896] textfile.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.rootsweb.com/~nwa/jeanne.html>.
2 CONT
2 CONT JEANNE LEMARCHAND -- LENEUF DU HERRISON
2 CONT Text by Gary Edwards
2 CONT
2 CONT My Greatx 11 Grandmother, Jeanne LeMarchand de La Celloniere and La Rocque arrived in New France in 1636 with her sons, Michel and Jacques LeNeuf. The Le Neuf family was the first noble family to settle permanently in New France. 
2 CONC Her eldest son, Michel became the Sieur du Herrisson and a Judge; the younger, Jacques was Sieur de La Potterie and was the Governor of Trois Riveres. The LeNeufs, along with their LeGardeur de Repentigny in-laws, were the most il
2 CONC lustrious family in the French colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne was married in St. Thury-Harcourt, Caen, Normandy, France by contract on December 1599 to Mathieu LeNeuf du Herrisson. The contract shows her parents as being Lt. Gervais Le Marchand, Sieur de La Celloniere and La Rocque an
2 CONC d Venote de St-Germain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The LeNeufs du Herrisson were given the opportunity in the 1630s to revitalize their dwindling fortunes (the seigneurie of Herrisson was not as prosperous as it was before the bourgeoise). They would be sent to New France to estab
2 CONC lish themselves (and the feudal aristocracy) in the name of the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT The LeNeuf brothers were very fair (the judgements passed down by Michel were very liberal) but rather hot-tempered. One day, Michel and Jacques were fighting with the habitants of Trois-Riveres. Jeanne, hearing the commotion, ste
2 CONC pped out of the Governor's mansion. She too got into the brawl and was punched in the stomach by Guillaume Isabel (he was not a habitant). Despite her noble upbringing, Jeanne apparently loved a fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty years later, her daughter-in-law, Marguerite LeNeuf de La Potterie born LeGardeur de Repentigny was found to have been the leader of the liqour trade with the Indians. The Le Neuf family was very much involved in the liquor
2 CONC  and fur trade, both of which made them fabulously wealthy by 17th century standards.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.biographi.ca/EN/ShowBio.asp?BioId=34471>:
2 CONT
2 CONT JACQUES LENEUF DE LA POTERIE, seigneur of Portneuf, governor of Trois-Rivières, acting governor of New France, brother of Michel Leneuf Du Hérisson; b. 1606 at Caen (Normandy); d. some time after 1685.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques Leneuf de La Poterie arrived at Quebec in 1636. In January of the same year he had managed to acquire the seigneury of Portneuf, a concession which was to be confirmed 16 April 1647. He likewise obtained, on 29 March 1649,
2 CONC  the marquisate of Sablé -- which despite its name was land held by a commoner -- the fief of the I'lle of Pigs (29 March 1649), the fief of La Poterie or Niverville (7 April 1660), which were lands situated in the region of Trois
2 CONC -Rivières, and also the seigneury of the Cap-des-Rosiers, in Gaspesia, which he shared with Charles Legardeur de Tilly and a few others.
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived at Trois-Rivières from 1640 on. On several occasions he was to act as deputy governor of this small town: from 17 Nov. 1645 to 2 Sept. 1648, then in 1650, 1652-53, 1658-62. On 13 May 1665 the Sovereign Council ordered a c
2 CONC ommission to be registered by virtue of which the governor Saffray de Mézy had nominated him "to be his Lieutenant after his demise". The governor had died on 5 May. The Council, on 27 May, refused to allow him the majority of the
2 CONC  prerogatives of this office, conceding him only the command of the militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 Oct. 1666 Jacques Leneuf sailed on the Moulin dOr, on his way first to Acadia then to France. His titles of nobility were confirmed by Louis XIV in 1667 and registered in the Sovereign Council of New France in 1675. He was p
2 CONC ossibly back in Canada by 1667; in any case he was at Quebec 22 May 1668. Mention of his name is found in public documents up to 1685.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in France Jacques Leneuf had married Marguerite Legardeur, sister of Pierre Legardeur de Repentigny and of Charles Legardeur de Tilly, members of one of the most illustrious Canadian families. His son Michel Leneuf* de La Va
2 CONC llière, born in October 1640, was to play an important role in Acadia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leneuf de La Poterie had always been interested in the fur trade -- as well as in the trafficking of spirits. He was one of the members of the "Communauté des Habitants". He was a wily business man who often had brushes with the l
2 CONC aw.
2 CONT
2 CONT Léopold Lamontagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Édits ord., II, 25f. JR (Thwaites), VIII, 220; IX, 142; XVIII 90; LXXIII, 79. JJ (Laverdière et Casgrain). Jug. et délib., I, 344, 347, 350, 436, 996, 1001; II, 1045. Lettres de noblesse (P.-G. Roy). Papier terrier de la Cie des I
2 CONC .O. (P.-G. Roy). P.-G. Roy, Inv. concessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT BRH, II (1896), 67-69; VI (1900), 29; IX (1903), 160, 311-14; XXI (1915), 46. Raymond Douville, "La dictature de la famille Le Neuf," Cahiers des Dix, XX (1955), 61-89. Hugolin Lemay, Le père Joseph Denis, premier récollet canadie
2 CONC n (1657-1736) (2v., Québec, 1926), I, 40f. Sulte, Mélanges historiques (Malchelosse), XVIII, 6f., 9, 19 ; "Premiers seigneurs du Canada, 1634-1664," RSCT, 1st ser., I (1882-83), sect.i, 131-37. Tanguay, Dictionnaire, I, 381. E. Va
2 CONC illancourt, La conquête du Canada par les Normands (Montréal, 1933).
2 CONT
2 CONT ? 2000 University of Toronto/Université Laval
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.biographi.ca/EN/ShowBio.asp?BioId=35037> is the son of the above parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT MICHEL LENEUF DE LA VALLIRE DE BEAUBASSIN, (the elder), captain, commandant and governor of Acadia, major, seigneur of Beaubassin; baptized 31 Oct. 1640 at Trois-Rivières, third son of Jacques Leneuf* de La Poterie, governor of th
2 CONC at town, and of Marguerite Legardeur; d. in 1705.
2 CONT
2 CONT After studying in France, La Vallière returned to Canada in 1657. According to Charlevoix* he was in command on I"lle Royale (Cape Breton Island) in 1666, under Nicolas Denys*. His first marriage, probably in 1666, was to Marie, o
2 CONC nly daughter of Denys, and his second, around 1683, was to Françoise, daughter of Simon Denys* de La Trinité. He had eight children, all by his first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1672 he is supposed to have set up a fur-trading post on the isthmus of Chignecto, while devoting part of his time to the fishing industry, farming, settlement, and soldiering. On 24 Oct. 1676 Buade* de Frontenac granted him a 
2 CONC piece of land 10 square leagues in area, constituting the Beaubassin seigneury. Later this region was to become one of the strategic points in the struggles between French and English in Acadia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7 May 1676, on Frontenacs orders, he set off to cruise along the Acadian coasts to spy on the enemy. While thus occupied, with his brother-in-law Sieur Richard Denys* de Fronsac as his second in command, he seized three English
2 CONC  ketches from Boston that were taking on coal at Cape Breton: two of them were declared lawful prizes.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Vallière was promoted commandant of Acadia in 1678, replacing Pierre de Joybert*. He enjoyed the favour of Frontenac, who in 1681 recommended him to the minister as a future governor, being "a nobleman who has all the qualities
2 CONC  of mind and heart necessary to acquit himself well in such a post." Duchesneau* looked with disapproval on this protection, which subsequently was the cause of a disagreement between Duchesneau and Frontenac. Appointed governor i
2 CONC n 1683, La Vallière exercised his functions for one year only at Port-Royal (Annapolis Royal, N.S.), after which he returned to his residence at Beaubassin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The numerous differences in which he was involved appear to have done him harm. The most serious one was with Bergier*, who since 1682 had been running the Compagnie de la Pêche sédentaire de lAcadie. Bergier reproached him with c
2 CONC reating difficulties for his undertaking by distributing fishing licences too readily to Bostonians, thus preparing the way for the loss of Acadia. Despite everything, Intendant de Meulles continued to support La Vallière, but the
2 CONC  court, yielding to the companys representations, replaced him with François-Marie Perrot* who was named governor in April 1684.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Vallière maintained good relations with the governor of Boston, Simon Bradstreet; on 8 August 1684 he expressed to him his regret on learning from Bergier, who had made a trip to France, that trade between the two colonies woul
2 CONC d shortly be forbidden.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1685 he had the honour of receiving Jacques de Meulles at Beaubassin, on an official visit to Acadian soil. The intendant had to spend the winter with La Vallière, whom he considered to be the person best qualifie
2 CONC d to give him information about the colony. It was thanks to La Vallières little sailing-boat, the Saint-Antoine, which was assigned to coastal traffic between Port-Royal and Baie Française (Bay of Fundy), that de Meulles was able
2 CONC  to go to Port-Royal in the spring of 1686. On Brisay de Denonvilles orders, La Vallière went to France in the autumn to report on the situation in Acadia. On that occasion Denonville expressed to the minister his doubts about the
2 CONC  charges levelled against La Vallière in Acadia.
2 CONT
2 CONT During 1687 La Vallière entrusted his seigneurial domain to his future son-in-law, Claude-Sébastien de Villieu, and came back to Canada. He was made a midshipman in April of the same year, and received a double promotion in 1689: 
2 CONC in June, lieutenant in Acadia, then in October, captain of Frontenacs guards. At the time of the siege of Quebec by Phips*, in 1690, he was responsible for the exchange of prisoners at Pointe de Lévis, in particular Pierre Bécart 
2 CONC de Granville and Abbe Trouvé. He performed this task so well that Frontenac recommended him for promotion to the rank of major.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1691 he became a captain in place of M. dEscairac (Desquerac), and in August 1695 he was left in command of Fort Cataracoui (Frontenac) with 48 men; his orders were to try to reach an accord with the Iroquois. He returned to Qu
2 CONC ebec in the spring of 1696, and in June embarked on the Bouffonne, with two of his sons, Alexandre and Jacques, and Charles Bécart de Fonville as officers, and with a crew of 150, in order to go to harass the enemy along the coast
2 CONC s of Acadia. It was in September of the same year that Church attacked Beaubassin and laid it to waste.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king appointed La Vallière town major of Montreal on 28 May 1699. In the autumn Callière entrusted him and Father Bruyas with a diplomatic mission to the governor of Boston, Bellomont; he was to discuss the repatriation of Fre
2 CONC nch prisoners and to ascertain his attitude towards the Indians. In 1700, according to a joint report by Callière and Bochart de Champigny to the minister, dated 6 November, La Vallière was in bad financial straits, and they recom
2 CONC mended him to the ministers protection. Two years later the king granted him a gratuity of 500 livres to set up in Acadia an establishment for porpoise fishing, but it is not believed that La Vallière ever carried out this plan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1704 La Vallière returned to France, bearing official letters from Rigaud de Vaudreuil and François de Beauharnois* de La Chaussaye. They delegated him to go because of his profound knowledge of Canadas needs, of 
2 CONC the Indians, and of the troops; also according to them, because he could expect to be favourably received by the minister. There is every reason to believe that he took advantage of this opportunity to solicit the ratification of 
2 CONC the original grant of his seigneurial domain of Beaubassin (1676), which was not in fact ratified until 2 June 1705. This decree confirmed La Vallières seigneurial claims to Beaubassin and Chipoudy, at the same time recognizing th
2 CONC e rights of occupation of the original settlers of these places. Thus ended the disputes which two groups of Acadians from Port-Royal had carried on with him; one group, led by the surgeon Jacques Bourgeois, had settled at Beaubas
2 CONC sin before La Vallière, and the other led by Pierre Tibaudeau had arrived at Chipoudy in 1698.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Vallières death probably occurred in July 1705 while he was crossing from France to Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michel Leneuf de La Vallière had an eventful career, for he was at one and the same time a sailor, traveller, fur-trader, seigneur, colonizer, diplomat, and governor. This deserving native of Trois-Rivières, Frontenacs confidentia
2 CONC l agent, left his work at Beaubassin unfinished; it is regrettable that none of his four sons continued it.
2 CONT
2 CONT J.-Roger Comeau
2 CONT
2 CONT [Some authors have claimed that La Vallière took part in an expedition headed for Hudson Bay in 1661, with Fathers Dablon* and Druillettes* (see Jean Delanglez, Life and voyages of Louis Jolliet (Chicago, 1948), 151, and Marie de 
2 CONC Saint-Jean dArs, "A la recherche de la mer du Nord, 1661," RHAF, VIII (1954-55), 220-35). P.-G. Roy affirmed this at first (see BRH, XXII (1916), 26), but later stated that La Vallière could not have been present (Les officiers dé
2 CONC tat-major, 126-28). The account of the journey in JR (Thwaites), XLVI, 252ff., does not mention La Vallière. Thus there seems to be little evidence that he took part in the expedition.  j.-r.c.]
2 CONT
2 CONT AN, Col., B, 8, f.30v; 15, f.83; 20, f.228v; 22, f.99; 23, f.64; 25, ff.117, 117v; C11A, 5, ff.268, 274, 304; 8, ff.150-51; 11, ff.92, 97, 221; 13, f.379; 14, ff.16, 185-86; 18, f.24; 22, ff.23, 341; C11D, 1, f.148; 5, ff.10, 81-8
2 CONC 3; E, 277 (dossier de La Vallière), pièces 7, 55; F3, 4, ff.49394; Section Outre-Mer, G1, 466 (Recensement de lAcadie, 1686). AQ, NF, Coll. de pièces jud. et not., liasse 5, pièce 242. PAC, MG 9, B 9, 2. PRO, C.O. 1/55, 374. Acadi
2 CONC ensia Nova (Morse), I, 24-26, 92f.; II, 56-58. Charlevoix, Histoire (1744), I, 388. Coll. de manuscrits relatifs à la N.-F., I, 291-96, 298-99. "Correspondance de Frontenac (1672-82)," APQ Rapport, 1926-27, 111, 137. NYCD (OCallag
2 CONC han and Fernow), IX, 304. Webster, Acadia, 206-8.
2 CONT
2 CONT P.-G. Roy, Les officiers détat-major, 13, 124-46. Tanguay, Dictionnaire, I, 381. Bernard, Le drame acadien, 128-48. Rameau de Saint-Père, Une colonie féodale, I, 145, 167-82, 243-46, 267-70. Azarie Couillard Després, "Les gouverne
2 CONC urs de lAcadie sous le régime français, 1600-1710," RSCT, 3d ser., XXXIII (1939), sect.i, 263-65. Philéas Gagnon, "Noms propres au Canada français," 121. Ganong, "Historic sites in New Brunswick," 278, 304-5, 315-16. B. T. McCully
2 CONC , "The New England-Acadia fishery dispute and the Nicholson mission of August, 1687," Essex Institute, Salem, Mass., Hist. Coll., XCVI (1960), 278-80.
2 CONT
