1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Golden Fleece - Knights: Spanish Branch====
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Louis_Hector_de_Villars '''Wikipedia....''' - English]   
2 CONT * [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Louis_Hector_de_Villars '''Wikipedia....''' - Français] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude Louis Hector de Villars, Prince de Martigues, Marquis then Duc de Villars, Vicomte de Melun (8 May 1653 – 17 June 1734) was the last great general of Louis XIV of France and one of the most brilliant commanders in French mi
2 CONC litary history,  one of only six Marshals who have been promoted to '''Marshal General of France'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Early Career====
2 CONT Villars was born at Moulins (in the present-day département of Allier) in a noble but poor family - his father was the diplomat Pierre de Villars. He entered the French army through the corps of pages in 1671. He distinguished him
2 CONC self at twenty in the Siege of Maastricht in 1673 during the Franco-Dutch War and after the bloody Battle of Seneffe a year later he was promoted on the field to mestre de camp (colonel) of a cavalry regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next promotion would take time in spite of a long record of excellent service under Turenne, The Great Condé and Luxembourg, and of his aristocratic birth. The reason was that he had incurred the enmity of the powerful Louvois
2 CONC , he was finally made maréchal de camp in 1687.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the interval between the Dutch wars and the formation of the League of Augsburg, Villars, who combined with his military gifts the tact and subtlety of the diplomatist, was employed in an unofficial mission to the court of Bava
2 CONC ria, and there became the constant companion of Maximilian II Emanuel, Elector of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned to France in 1690 and was given a command in the cavalry of the army in Flanders, but towards the end of the War of the Grand Alliance he went to Vienna as ambassador.
2 CONT ====War of the Spanish Succession====
2 CONT It was Villars' part in the next war, beginning with Friedlingen (1702) and Hochstadt (1703) and ending with Denain (1712), that has made him most famous. For his part in the battle of Friedlingen he received the marshalate, and f
2 CONC or the pacification of the insurgent Cévennes he received the Saint-Esprit order and the title of duke. Friedlingen and Hochstadt were barren victories, and the campaigns of which they formed were characterized by lost opportuniti
2 CONC es. Villars' glory thus begins with the year 1709 when France, apparently helpless, was roused to a great effort of self-defence by the stringent demands of the Coalition.
2 CONT
2 CONT In that year he was called to command the main army opposing Prince Eugène of Savoy and Marlborough on the northern frontier. During the famine of the winter he shared the soldiers' miserable rations. When the campaign opened the 
2 CONC old Marshal Boufflers volunteered to serve under him, and after the terrible battle of Malplaquet, in which he was gravely wounded (by a musketball to the knee), he was able to tell the king: "If it please God to give your majesty
2 CONC 's enemies another such victory, they are ruined."
2 CONT
2 CONT Two more campaigns passed without a battle and with scarcely any advance on the part of the invaders, but at last Marlborough manoeuvred Villars out of the famous Ne plus ultra lines, and the power of the defence seemed to be brok
2 CONC en. But Louis made a last effort, the English contingent and its great leader were withdrawn from the enemy's camp, and Villars, though still suffering from his Malplaquet wounds, outmanoeuvered and decisively defeated Eugène at D
2 CONC enain. This victory saved France, though the war dragged on for another year on the Rhine, where Villars took Landau, led the stormers at Freiburg and negotiated the Treaty of Rastatt with Prince Eugène.
2 CONT ====Regency====
2 CONT Villars played a conspicuous part in the politics of the Regency period as the principal opponent of Cardinal Dubois, and only the memories of Montmorency's rebellion prevented his being made constable of France. He took the field
2 CONC  for the last time in the War of the Polish Succession (1734), with the title marshal-general of the king's armies, that Turenne had held before him. But he was over eighty years old at this point, and after opening the campaign e
2 CONC nergetically he Turenne he died at Turin on 17 June 1734. Coat of Arms of duc de Villars in '''Vaux-le-Vicomte castle'''
2 CONT ====Legacy====
2 CONT Villars's memoirs show us a fanfaron plein d'honneur, as Voltaire calls him. He was indeed boastful, and also covetous of honours and wealth. But he was an honourable man of high courage, moral and physical, and a soldier who stan
2 CONC ds above all his contemporaries and successors in the 18th century, on the same height as Maurice de Saxe and Frederick II of Prussia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The memoirs, part of which was published in 1734 and afterwards several times republished in untrustworthy versions, were for the first time completely edited by the Marquis de Vogüé in 1884–92.
