2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 337 Identified as a native of Ste. Anne's parish in Canada in HBC accounts, 27 years old with 12 years of service in
2 CONT 1830 (HBCA, B.135/g/11). De la Ronde had engaged with the North West Company in 1818, and was posted to "Lac des Isles" north and west of Lake Nipigon. The De la Rondes had ancestors in minor French nobility, including Louis Denys
2 CONC  de la Ronde (1675 - 1741), seigneur and commander of the French post of Chagouamigon (near present-day Ashland, Wisconsin). This de la Ronde was one of the first Europeans to investigate the development of copper mines on Lake Su
2 CONC perior. A Denys de la Ronde held a permit to trade with the Indians at "La Prairie des François" in 1781, and a "Chevallier Laronde" held a permit for Michilimackinac. Louis Denis de Laronde, Louis LaRonde's father, was a trader o
2 CONC n Lake Nipissing in the late eighteenth century; Louis LaRonde's mother was Louis Denis' second wife, a Louise King of Montréal. Louis married the daughter of an Indian called "The Pelican" around Osnaburgh House in 1827. Louis' u
2 CONC ncle Charles was also a fur trader and married Madeline Pewadjiwonokwe from Lake Simcoe. A half-brother, Toussaint Laronde, one of ten children from Louis LaRonde's father's first marriage to a Nipissing Indian woman, was an inter
2 CONC preter for the North West Company in the Fond du Lac Department in 1805 ("List of the men &co at the Fond du Lac Department", in Roderick Mackenzie, "Some Account of the North West Company"). See Elizabeth Arthur, "The de Larondes
2 CONC  of Lake Nipigon", in Thunder Bay Historical Museum Society, Paper and Records, Vol. IX 1981, 31 - 48; Thunder Bay Museum and Archives, miscellaneous genealogical material related to the De la Ronde family The Historical Roots of 
2 CONC Métis Communities North of Lake Superior Gwynneth C. D. Jones Vancouver, B. C. 31 March 2015. Prepared for the Métis Nation of Ontario http://www.metisnation.org/media/586242/mno_report_on_historic_metis_north_of_lake_superior_(ma
2 CONC rch2015).pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT DENYS DE LA RONDE, LOUIS (he signed La Ronde Denys), officer in the navy and in the colonial regular troops; b. 2 Aug. 1675 at Quebec, son of Pierre Denys* de La Ronde and Catherine Leneuf de La Poterie; m. 20 July 1709 at Quebec,
2 CONC  Marie-Louise, daughter of René-Louis Chartier* de Lotbinière, and first cousin of Louise-Élisabeth Joybert*, wife of the governor-general of New France, Philippe de Rigaud* de Vaudreuil; had three sons and three daughters; buried
2 CONC  25 March 1741 at Quebec.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Denys de La Ronde entered the naval service as a midshipman at Rochefort, France, in 1687. For the next 20 years he served in a wide variety of campaigns: he accompanied the deposed English king James II to Ireland in 1689, 
2 CONC and saw action there at Bantry Bay and again off Beachy Head, England; in eight campaigns to New France between 1692 and 1705, serving several times under his older brother, Simon-Pierre Denys* de Bonaventure, he came to know the 
2 CONC New England coastline well; in 1697 he accompanied Pierre Le Moyne* d’Iberville on his final and most dramatic campaign to Hudson Bay; and in 1699–1700 and 1701, at the specific request of d’Iberville, he served in the Mississippi
2 CONC  expeditions. He was given his first command in November 1701, when d’Iberville entrusted him with the Enflammé for the return voyage to France. The following year, on his way to Quebec, he suffered a serious shoulder wound in an 
2 CONC action against an English vessel. Twice, in 1695 and 1704, he was taken prisoner by the English on the high seas.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Ronde began his colonial service in 1705 when he was given command of a small frigate to be used against English shipping in Acadian and New England waters. To counter France’s increasing inability to meet the wartime requireme
2 CONC nts of the colony, the governor of Acadia, Jacques-François Monbeton* de Brouillan, had decided to have the Biche, 18 to 20 guns, built at Port-Royal (Annapolis Royal, N.S.). Finding the vessel unfinished when he arrived, La Ronde
2 CONC  took charge of a brig and began privateering and reconnoitring English strength along the New England seaboard. In 1706 he went so far as to put in at Boston “on the pretext of making inquiries about developments . . . regarding 
2 CONC exchanges [of prisoners].” It may have been on this occasion that he made contacts which later would be useful to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just as the Biche, with a crew of 60 Canadians under Charles-Joseph Amiot* de Vincelotte, was ready to sail from Port-Royal in the spring of 1707, a force of New England provincials under John March* arrived before the town. The e
2 CONC nergy of the new governor, Daniel d’Auger* de Subercase, aided substantially by La Ronde and his men, compelled the New Englanders to withdraw; La Ronde carried news of the successful defence to the court. While in France he had t
2 CONC he Biche replaced by a larger frigate, the Vénus, and after an arduous crossing he returned to Port-Royal on Easter Sunday 1708. Anticipating a new English attack, Subercase ordered him to take up his command of the infantry compa
2 CONC ny awarded him in 1707 following the resignation of Louis-Simon Le Poupet* de La Boularderie.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was only late in August therefore – and despite the desertion of the greater part of his crew – that La Ronde was able to resume his privateering. Taking on 40 men recently arrived from the West Indies, he set out from Port-Roy
2 CONC al on the Vénus and promptly captured two prizes worth more than 115,000 livres. But contrary winds forced him to put in at Plaisance (Placentia, Nfld.), where the governor, Philippe Pastour* de Costebelle, persuaded him to join a
2 CONC n expedition against the English forts at St John’s. Leaving Port-Royal to its fate, La Ronde carried munitions and supplies to St John’s by sea, while troops under Saint-Ovide [Monbeton] proceeded overland. On New Year’s Day 1709
2 CONC , barely hours after the French arrived, the English under Thomas Lloyd* surrendered. When the station was abandoned by the French in the spring and the forts destroyed, La Ronde carried the artillery back to Plaisance aboard the 
2 CONC Vénus. He then agreed to transport the English garrison of St John’s to Quebec and, once there, stood by in anticipation of the massive British invasion expected for 1709. It was on this occasion that he married Marie-Louise Chart
2 CONC ier de Lotbinière. When it became apparent that Canada would enjoy a respite in 1709, La Ronde returned with his bride to Plaisance in October.
2 CONT
2 CONT As early as 1708 Subercase had complained bitterly not only of La Ronde’s insubordination in not returning to Port-Royal but of his mistreatment of minor officials who had opposed his attempts that year to undervalue his captured 
2 CONC prizes, thereby cheating the government, which was owed taxes on the vessels, as well as the outfitters of his ship. Although he agreed that La Ronde ought to be severely punished, the minister of Marine, Pontchartrain, faced a di
2 CONC lemma: “I really do not know how to deal with him,” he remarked, “since he acquitted himself so well at St John’s.” La Ronde returned to France in 1710 to account for his conduct. Though at first glance he appears to have withstoo
2 CONC d the wrath of authorities, the balance of his career was adversely affected by his indiscipline. Despite his advancement at the normal rate (section leader of a detachment of midshipmen at 26, sub-lieutenant in the navy at 27, ca
2 CONC ptain in the colonial regulars at 32), and despite a truly impressive record of service, he never rose beyond the rank of captain. He did, however, receive the honour of the cross of Saint-Louis in 1721.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1710 La Ronde sailed for Port-Royal only to learn before arriving of its surrender to Francis Nicholson* in October. He therefore stayed at Plaisance, much to the delight of his patron Costebelle, by whom he was soon to be disp
2 CONC atched on his most spectacular adventure. The following spring La Ronde, who, Costebelle claimed, “knows enough English to understand it and to make himself understood without an interpreter,” went to Boston under a flag of truce,
2 CONC  ostensibly to negotiate an exchange of prisoners. In reality he was to make careful military observations, seeking at the same time, surreptitiously and with “great caution,” to subvert the fighting spirit of the New Englanders. 
2 CONC He was to suggest “in conversation” that by subscribing to the cause of Old England in America, the colonists were in effect using “their own weapons for the destruction of the freedom of their republics.”
2 CONT
2 CONT There was apparently no opposition to La Ronde’s presence in Boston, in spite of the fact that preparations were being made for a massive invasion of Canada. He may already have had contacts there, and in any case he was a persona
2 CONC ble man who made a favourable impression despite his uneasiness in his role. Sir Hovenden Walker* must have thought him amenable for he suggested that he aid the British cause by piloting a vessel to the St Lawrence – an offer La 
2 CONC Ronde was probably not above accepting for a price. It was not long, however, before a copy of his instructions was discovered. Markedly less welcome now and anxious, as he later claimed, to get his news to the French, La Ronde ma
2 CONC de a dash by night for the safety of the open sea. In the first of many difficulties which “endangered his life several times,” he was stopped and thrown into the dungeon of Castle William. Although he had had the foresight to swa
2 CONC llow the original text of his instructions, the General Court of Massachusetts summarily condemned him to hang. In the meantime, however, news of Walker’s disaster had reached Boston. Out of compassion, or influenced perhaps by La
2 CONC  Ronde’s colourful personality, Governor Joseph Dudley intervened and gave the Frenchman the opportunity to make his escape. Otherwise he would have paid dearly for his rash plunge into the vagaries of international diplomacy in w
2 CONC artime. Late in 1711, having been “tossed about on the sea like a latterday Telemachus,” he drifted into Martinique. He returned to Plaisance in July 1712.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the spring of 1713 the peace of Utrecht had ceded Acadia and Newfoundland to England, and in an effort to counter the loss France decided upon a new venture on Île Royale (Cape Breton Island). Serving under Saint-Ovide, La Rond
2 CONC e reconnoitred the island, making recommendations for settlement and preparing charts and reports of his observations. It is perhaps significant that he noted with some emotion the vestiges of the pioneering efforts of his grandfa
2 CONC ther, Simon Denys* de La Trinité, who more than 60 years previously had built a trading post at Sainte-Anne (Englishtown). In the summer of the following year La Ronde joined Jacques d’Espiet* de Pensens on an unsuccessful journey
2 CONC  to Nova Scotia to encourage the Acadians to leave the province and immigrate to Île Royale.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Ronde carried the colony’s dispatches to France in January 1715. Upon his return he commanded the detachment at Port-Toulouse (St Peters) intermittently from November 1715 to the spring of 1720. In 1719, at the request of Gover
2 CONC nor General Philippe de Rigaud de Vaudreuil and seemingly against his own wishes, he was appointed to the garrison in Canada. By procrastinating, however, he missed the last vessel to Quebec in 1719. In 1720 Robert-David Gottevill
2 CONC e* de Belile, sent by the Comte de Saint-Pierre to command at Île Saint-Jean (Prince Edward Island), secured from Saint-Ovide, now governor of Île Royale, the services of an unenthusiastic La Ronde to assist in the founding of the
2 CONC  colony. In November 1721 La Ronde prepared a detailed mémoire on their progress. Its reliability is highly suspect, however, for it seems that La Ronde was more interested in promoting himself as a colonizer than in reporting acc
2 CONC urately. In 1722 he took advantage of some administrative confusion at Versailles to leave Île Saint-Jean and take up the Canadian post Vaudreuil had held for him since 1719.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Ronde, with his knowledge of English and his contacts in New England, proved especially useful to Vaudreuil as a plenipotentiary. In 1723, for example, the governor sent him overland to Boston on a diplomatic mission, the preci
2 CONC se purpose of which is unclear. He also impressed Vaudreuil’s successor, Charles de Beauharnois, who appointed him, on 6 June 1727, commandant of Chagouamigon (near Ashland, Wis.), a key post on the route to the far west. Even tho
2 CONC ugh his appointment included the right to trade for furs, for unknown reasons La Ronde did not immediately depart from Quebec.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1728 he became embroiled in the power struggle between Beauharnois and the intendant, Claude-Thomas Dupuy*. Dupuy charged that La Ronde was involved in illegal fur trade, presumably with his merchant acquaintances in Boston, an
2 CONC d Beauharnois, forced to admit that the charge was true, refused him permission to go to Chagouamigon. The rare concurrence of the governor and intendant was apparently taken as proof of La Ronde’s guilt. After raising the possibi
2 CONC lity of dismissing him, in 1729 the minister of Marine, Maurepas, ordered Beauharnois to keep La Ronde at Quebec: “I cannot have any confidence in him,” he wrote. For La Ronde the possibility of further promotion was finally elimi
2 CONC nated, but he was able, thanks chiefly to his friendship with Dupuy’s successor, Gilles Hocquart*, to take up his command at Chagouamigon in 1731.
2 CONT
2 CONT There a new chapter in his spectacular career unfolded. Having learned from the area’s Indians and from Jacques Legardeur de Saint-Pierre, a veteran officer in the region, that several islands in Lake Superior held rich copper dep
2 CONC osits, he began to gather samples and to lay plans for a major industry. In 1732 he wrote to Beauharnois and Hocquart with a proposal to construct two ships on lakes Superior and Huron to carry ore to Niagara (near Youngstown, N.Y
2 CONC .); there it would be loaded on barges for the voyage to Montreal. In return he asked for a free nine-year lease on the fur trade at Chagouamigon to help him finance the venture. After Maurepas approved the plan in 1733 La Ronde f
2 CONC ormed a partnership with Saint-Pierre and Louis-Mathieu Damours de Clignancour, agreeing to put up half the capital. Since he apparently did not have it, he borrowed 25,411 livres in merchandise from the Montreal merchant Louis Ch
2 CONC arly Saint-Ange. In 1734 he drew another merchant, Simon Guillory, into the partnership. Meanwhile, Beauharnois and Hocquart modified the terms of his monopoly, allowing him to forgo the construction of the Lake Huron vessel until
2 CONC  1735 and to use the king’s barque on Lake Ontario.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lake Superior vessel was constructed by La Ronde and Guillory in 1734. The ore samples they sent to France were assayed at the royal mint in Paris as more than 90 per cent pure copper and led Maurepas to believe that “the vent
2 CONC ure could become quite important.” La Ronde, who, as Hocquart observed, possessed only “ordinary resourcefulness, which is not supported by any practical knowledge,” realized that expert artisans would be needed to select the best
2 CONC  location for the mine and to set up the smelting furnace. In 1735 he petitioned Maurepas to send help from France at his expense. Maurepas chose instead to send two German miners, John Adam and Christopher Henry Forster, with ins
2 CONC tructions merely to assess the venture’s feasibility. The Forsters arrived in Canada in the late summer of 1737 but, as a result of various mishaps, did not reach Lake Superior until June 1738. After investigating the copper depos
2 CONC its and taking ore samples, they returned to Quebec in the early summer of 1739 where they also examined copper deposits at Baie Saint-Paul before sailing for France. Their report on La Ronde’s venture confirmed the existence of a
2 CONC t least three rich copper deposits on Lake Superior but declared that the cost of exploiting them would be prohibitive.
2 CONT
2 CONT La Ronde anticipated the effect of this report in France. In 1739 he wrote to Maurepas painting a bright picture of the mining enterprise and requesting a number of artisans, but his dispatch was couched in defensive terms. It sou
2 CONC ght to justify all his actions since 1736 and contained a lengthy account of his services since 1687. But Maurepas replied in 1740 that the enterprise was dead and angrily ordered La Ronde to reimburse the king for the sums advanc
2 CONC ed to the Forsters, more than 6,000 livres. He apparently did so, and Beauharnois and Hocquart requested that he be permitted to exploit the fur trade at Chagouamigon for two or three years so that he could also pay his private cr
2 CONC editors. The certainty of the Forsters’ report cast doubt upon La Ronde’s sincerity about the mining venture; that the costs of labour and transportation would be prohibitive was so clear to them that La Ronde, even with his limit
2 CONC ed expertise, might have been expected to be aware of the problem. Moreover, in the seven years that he led the authorities to believe in the enterprise he achieved little, aside from building a ship on Lake Superior which would t
2 CONC ransport furs and trade goods as readily as ore. That his private financial support came from fur merchants who were all linked with the far western trade is also suspicious. Official documents suggest that La Ronde took the ventu
2 CONC re seriously, that he suffered losses, and that he was finally defeated by Canada’s geography, but it is worth noting that they can be given a different interpretation. It is at least a possibility that La Ronde used the mining ve
2 CONC nture as a means of obtaining a free lease on the fur trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the failure of the mining enterprise, La Ronde sought promotion to commandant of the colonial regular troops in New France, as well as promotions for two of his sons, Philippe and Pierre-François-Paul, both of whom were offi
2 CONC cers. Philippe had served at Chagouamigon during his father’s absences and took over permanently when La Ronde died in 1741. In 1743 Mme de La Ronde went to France to ask for a further extension of her husband’s lease so that she 
2 CONC and her sons could pay Charly the 30,000 livres she maintained were still owed him. This request may have sounded disingenuous since she and her children had earlier renounced their inheritance to avoid paying a 1,100 livres debt 
2 CONC to the Canadian treasury agent, Thomas-Jacques Taschereau. Even so she was granted the fur ferme at Chagouamigon from 1744 to 1748 at 3,000 livres, and one-third of its product from 1748 to 1751.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is difficult to assess La Ronde’s career. He was without doubt a daring soldier of fortune whose exploits covered the French Atlantic empire and ranged from heroic military feats to diplomacy, exploration, and entrepreneurship.
2 CONC  On the other hand he was guilty of insubordination, apparently guided by self-interest, and often suspected, at least, of treachery. The many mysteries that surround him, concerning not only his motives for the mining venture but
2 CONC  his contacts in New England and his alleged collusion with them, only add to the fascination of his career.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard Pothier and Donald J. Norton
2 CONT
2 CONT AJQ, Registre d’état civil, Notre-Dame de Québec, 4 août 1675. AN, Col., B, 21, 29, 30, 32, 35, 37, 41, 42, 56–75; C11A, 53–87; C11B, 1–5, 12; C11C, 4, 6, 7, 11, 15; C11D, 4–7; C11G, 2–3; D2C, 222/2; E, 119, 189. BN, mss, Clairamb
2 CONC ault, 875, ff.245–47. “Mass. Archives,” II, V, XXXVIIIA. PRO, CO 218/1. Édits ord., II, 373–74. Fauteux, Les chevaliers de Saint-Louis. “Genealogy of the de la Ronde family,” J. E. Ducros, comp. (typescript, Mandeville, La., 1938)
2 CONC , 14–15. Le Jeune, Dictionnaire, I, 79–81. A. Roy, Inv. greffes not., IV, 31–32; XVIII, 170; XIX, 208, 282. P.-G. Roy, Inv. concessions, IV, 263–64; Inv. jug. et délib., 1717–1760, II, 60, 128, 215, 225; III, 138–39. Dubé, Claude-
2 CONC Thomas Dupuy, 269. J.-N. Fauteux, Essai sur l’industrie, II, 10–20. Frégault, La civilisation de la Nouvelle-France. Harvey, French régime in P.E.I., 43–50. Kellogg, French régime, 351. La Morandière, Hist. de la pêche française, 
2 CONC II, 646, 695ff. Robert Le Blant, Un colonial sous Louis XIV: Philippe de Pastour de Costebelle, gouverneur de Terre-Neuve puis de l’île Royale, 1661–1717 (Paris, Dax, 1935), 125, 149. McLennan, Louisbourg, 11–17, 32, 37. Nish, Les
2 CONC  bourgeoisgentilshommes, 90, 111. H.-R. Casgrain, “Éclaircissements sur la question acadienne,” RSCT, 1st ser., VI (1888), sect.i, 23–75. Henri Têtu, “Le chapitre de la cathédrale de Québec et ses délégués en France,” BRH, XVI (19
2 CONC 10), 321.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cite This Article Bernard Pothier and Donald J. Norton, “DENYS DE LA RONDE, LOUIS,” in Dictionary of Canadian Biography, vol. 3, University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–, accessed June 5, 2020, http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/
2 CONC denys_de_la_ronde_louis_3E.html.
2 CONT
2 CONT The citation above shows the format for footnotes and endnotes according to the Chicago manual of style (16th edition). Information to be used in other citation formats:
2 CONT
2 CONT Permalink:  	http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/denys_de_la_ronde_louis_3E.html Author of Article:  	Bernard Pothier and Donald J. Norton Title of Article:  	DENYS DE LA RONDE, LOUIS Publication Name:  	Dictionary of Canadian Biograph
2 CONC y, vol. 3 Publisher:  	University of Toronto/Université Laval Year of revision:  	1974 Access Date:  	June 5, 2020
2 CONT
2 CONT French nval officer, prospector. As a young naval officer he accompanied James II to Ireland in 1687. By 1692 he was fighting for the French in North America. He accompanied Iberville to both Hudson Bay and to the Mississippi. He 
2 CONC worked the coast of New England for a number of years causing much trounle for shipping in and out of Boston, and thus became a hunted man. He was taken prisoner of the British twicw from the high seas, in 1695 and 1704. He learne
2 CONC d good English as a result of being a prisoner in Boston, and on various diplomatic missions. He never achieved a high rank due to his insubordination. In 1713 due to the Treaty of Utrecht, Acadia was given to the English. Isle St
2 CONC  Jean (P.E.I.) and the islands of Cape Breton (renamed ile Royale) were retained by the French. He was instrumental in the selection and setup of Louisbourg and Port Toulouse. He was sent west to become Commandant of Fort La Point
2 CONC e de Chequamegon, Madeleine Island, Wisconsin im1727, he learned of the Lake superior copper mines, and in 1733 received permission to work them.He operated the first sailing vessel on Lake superior and built up the settlement at 
2 CONC La Pointe in 1739.His mining hopes for the promising copper deposits were dashed by the outbreak of the Sioux-Chippewa war in 1740. Thus ended his hopes for one of the first mining companies in North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1713, under the Utrecht Peace Treaty, Acadia and Newfoundland were ceded to England and, in an effort to compensate for its losses, France decided to embark on a new adventure on the island Royal (Cape Breton Island). La Ronde 
2 CONC recognized the island, making recommendations for settlement locations, mapping and reporting on sightings. "
2 CONT
2 CONT Seven years later, he was appointed as second to Governor Robert-David Gotteville Belile on Île Saint-Jean, Prince Edward Island, to help him found a colony there. In 1722, De la Ronde left Île Saint-Jean for Boston at the request
2 CONC  of Vaudreuil for a diplomatic mission, the purpose of which remained unclear. Then, Vaudreuil’s successor, Charles de Beauharnois appointed him commander of Chagouamigon (near Ashland, Wisc.) On June 6, 1727, a key fur trade post
2 CONC  on the western route. But he did not take this command until 1731. Now, in addition to the fur trade, the spy became a mining producer!
2 CONT
2 CONT "Having learned from the Indians of the region and from Jacques Legardeur de Saint-Pierre, a retired officer living on the spot, that the islands of Lake Superior were rich in copper deposits, he began to collect samples and draw 
2 CONC up plans for 'an important industry. This was followed by ten years of industrial activity, where shipbuilding and ore transportation were carried out at great cost. De La Ronde will partner with Legardeur de Saint-Pierre and Loui
2 CONC s-Mathieu Damours de Clignancour, agreeing with them to advance half the capital ... which he did not have. He will borrow 25,411 # of goods from a Montreal merchant, Louis Charly Saint-Ange.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mine will go into production, with a pure copper content of more than 90 percent. hundred. Minister Maurepas sends him two German miners, John Adam Forster and his son Christopher Henry, with instructions to determine the feas
2 CONC ibility of the business. "Their account of the La Ronde initiative confirmed the existence of at least three rich copper deposits at Lake Superior, but they considered that the cost of their exploitation would be prohibitive. "
2 CONT
2 CONT During the seven years that De la Ronde spent maintaining the authorities' hope for the success of his mining enterprise, "his only achievement was the construction of a vessel on Lake Superior capable of transporting both furs an
2 CONC d articles of only processes ore. The fact that he relied on financial contributions from fur traders all involved in Western trade is also suspect. Official documents suggest that La Ronde took this matter seriously, that he suff
2 CONC ered losses and that in the end he was defeated by the geography of Canada, but it must be added that the events of a other way. One possibility remains that La Ronde used mining as a pretext for obtaining a free fur trade concess
2 CONC ion. "
2 CONT
2 CONT After the failure of his mining business, La Ronde asked to be promoted to commanding officer in the Navy troops in New France, as well as a promotion for two of his sons, Philippe and Pierre-François-Paul, both officers. Philippe
2 CONC  had served in Chagouamigon during his father's absences and succeeded him after his death in 1741. In 1734, Mme de La Ronde went to France to ask for a further extension of her husband's concession, so that she and her sons may r
2 CONC eimburse Charly the 30,000 # which she claims was still due to her. (…) Her husband was hired to trade the furs in Chagouamigon for 3,000 livres, from 1744 to 1748, and for a third of his production, from 1748 to 1751.
2 CONT
2 CONT De La Ronde was a brave, active officer throughout the French Empire in America, a diplomat, an explorer and an entrepreneur (…) "The mystery that surrounds the motives underlying his mining initiatives as well as the contacts tha
2 CONC t he had in New England and the alleged collusion which linked him there, makes his career all the more fascinating. "
2 CONT
2 CONT From the DBC. http://www.claudeferland.com/2019/03/28/louis-denys-de-la-ronde-despion-industriel-et-marchand-2eme-de-2-articles/
