2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boschendal '''Boschendal''' is one of the oldest wine estates in South Africa and is located between Franschhoek and Stellenbosch in South Africa's Western Cape.
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm's title deeds are dated 1685, but this is likely to be a clerical mistake. The estate's first owner, Jean le Long, was one of the party of 200 French Huguenot refugees who were fleeing religious persecution in Europe. He 
2 CONC was granted land in the Cape of Good Hope by the Dutch East India Company in 1688 and the title deed was written in 1713. In 1715 the farm was acquired by another Huguenot, Abraham de Villiers who sold it to his brother Jacques in
2 CONC  1717. The de Villiers family farmed Boschendal until 1879. In 1812 Paul de Villiers and his wife, Anna Susanna Louw, completed a new house at Boschendal on the site of his father's home. This is the homestead as restored today.[1
2 CONC ] Among the guests in the later years of the de Villiers era was the British Governor at the Cape, Sir George Grey, who stayed at Boschendal whenever he visited the region.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''De Villiers Coat of Arms''' http://ancestry24.com/de-villiers-johan-hendrik/
2 CONT
2 CONT The de Villiers family coat of arms dates from the Middle Ages, possibly from the late 12th century. It is believed that a knight of the de Villiers family was awarded a coat of arms for exceptional services rendered to the French
2 CONC  royal family during the Crusades, which took place between the 11th century and late 13th century. The nobility of Europe emblazoned their shields with their own colourful identification marks as early as the 9th century. These m
2 CONC arks, which varied according to the customs of the different regions, were initially confined to shields, but later it was common to decorate also the helmets with coloured identification crests of feathers, especially for joustin
2 CONC g tournaments.
2 CONT
2 CONT It would seem that by the middle of the 12th century it had become customary to combine the emblazoned shields and the feathered crests, resulting in coats of arms as we know them to-day. The use of coats of arms was no longer lim
2 CONC ited to the nobility, but gradually spread to the knights and the emerging middle classes in the flourishing cities of Western Europe. A number of countries, including France, began keeping heraldic records during the second half 
2 CONC of the 12th century. Up to that time, it had been acceptable for a family to alter the design of its coat of arms from time to time, according to changed circumstances. When record-keeping was introduced, details of coats of arms 
2 CONC remained unaltered unless there were compelling reasons for change.
2 CONT
2 CONT The complete coat of arms is called an Achievement. The Achievement consists of five parts. The first is the crest on top of the helmet, which consisted of items that characterised a particular family, in this case a short sword (
2 CONC seax), held in a mailed fist.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second is the coronet or wreath which holds the crest and helmet together. The third is the mantling, representing the folds of the cloak of a knight. In the de Villiers family coat of arms the fourth part, the helmet, is an “
2 CONC esquire helmet”, indicating knighthood. The helmet is silver in colour and closed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The charges of the fifth part, the shield, are: (i) A red band on a silver background (upper part) and (ii) Agnus Dei or Lamb of God, sometimes referred to as the Paschal lamb, on a blue background (lower part). The lamb bears a s
2 CONC tandard. In France the shape of the heraldic shield was, from the 12th century onwards, always as depicted here.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Publications'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT A Walk Through Time
2 CONT http://www.scribd.com/doc/15507310/A-Walk-Through-Time
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 43 “At the Cape, at least two children were probably crippled at birth. These were Stephen, son of Martha Rousseau and Francois du Toit, and Jacob, the son of Susanne Gardiol and Abraham de Villiers.”  _______________________
2 CONC _______________________________________________________ AN ARCHAEOLOGY OF COLONIAL IDENTITY Contributions To Global Historical Archaeology, 2006, 67-118, DOI: 10.1007/0-306-48539-7_3Status and Settlement in the Cape Colony Subscri
2 CONC ber download available at http://www.springerlink.com/content/k9n43n4451825102/ ______________________________________________________________________________ Extracts from
2 CONT
2 CONT Full History of South Africa Under the Administration of the Dutch East India Company, 1652 to 1795" http://www.archive.org/stream/historysouthafr01theagoog/historysouthafr01theagoog_djvu.txt Page 336 “A few months after the first
2 CONC  party of Huguenots left the Netherlands a number of others were engaged to  come out as colonists. They embarked in the ships Wapen van Alkmaar and Zion. The first of these vessels  left Texel on the 27th of July 1688, and arrive
2 CONC d in Table Bay on the 27th of January 1689. She brought out  about forty immigrants, young and old. The Zion arrived on the 6th of May 1689, and in her came three brothers named Abraham, Pierre, and Jacob de Villiers, who were vin
2 CONC edressers from the neighbourhood of La Eochelle. “
2 CONT
2 CONT page 337 
2 CONT
2 CONT “Shortly after the refugees arrived in South Africa, the consistory of Batavia sent a sum of money equal to  twelve hundred and fifty English sovereigns to be distributed among them according to their needs. This money had constit
2 CONC uted the poor funds of a church at Formosa which was destroyed by an enemy, but the guardians managed to save their trust, and deposited it with the deacons at Batavia to be used for charitable purposes.  Nowadays 1,250/. may not 
2 CONC seem a very large amount, but if its purchasing power at that time be considered it will be found to have been a generous and noble gift, and it was appreciated as such by those whose wants it was intended to relieve. It was decid
2 CONC ed that all the Huguenots should share in this present, except a very few who were othervnse provided for. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The money was distributed on the 18th and 19th of April 1690, by commissioners who had taken every indi-  vidual's needs into consideration. A copy of the list of distribution is in the archives at the Hague, and it is  given here
2 CONC , as it contains the names of those who arrived in the Borssevburg, Zuid Beveland, and Wapen van Alkmaar, and shows further what havoc death had made in the little band of refugees previous to this date, with some other particular
2 CONC s. With a few names added from another docmnent, it forms a complete list of the Huguenots who settled in South Africa at this period. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Lombard, a sick man, with wife and one child 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Taillefer, with wife and four children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Jacob, with wife and three children . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Widow of Charles Marais, with four children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe Fouché, with wife and two children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham de Villiers, with wife and two brothers 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthieu Amiel, with wife and two children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hercnle da Pré with wife and five children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lonis Cordier, with wife and four children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean le Long, with wife and two children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Widow of Charles Préyot (remarried to Hendrik Eekhof), with four children by her deceased husband “
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 344
2 CONT
2 CONT “the request of the Huguenots to be located together was refused, and the government of the Cape was instructed, when granting ground, to mix the nationalities together so that they might speedily amalgamate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The despatch in which these resolutions were embodied reached the Cape in June 1691, and on the 80th of the following December the people of Drakenstein were formed into a separate congregation by the appointment of elders and dea
2 CONC cons from among themselves. The elders chosen by the council were Claude Marais, Louis de Berout, and Louis Cordier, and the deacons were Abraham de Villiers, Pierre Meyer, Pierre Beneset, and Pierre Bousseau. “
2 CONT
2 CONT 360 History of South Africa. [1691 
2 CONT
2 CONT “According to the census returns of 1691, corrected by entries in the church registers, the most notable burghers in the Cape district were: —“ page 691 “
2 CONT de Villiers, Abraham, with wife and two children,  de Villiers, Jacob, with wife and two children,  de Villiers, Pierre, “ _____________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Full text of "Old Cape Colony; a chronicle of her men and houses from 1652-1806" http://www.archive.org/stream/oldcapecolonychr00trot/oldcapecolonychr00trot_djvu.txt These are extracts from this publication – you are encouraged to
2 CONC  consult the full writing for extended context – a wonderful source! page 78
2 CONT
2 CONT “When a deputation, consisting of the minister Simond, Jacques de Savoy e, Abraham de Villiers, and two others, bearded (sic - ? ,meaning CJB) the 
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 79 OLD CAPE COLONY 
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor at the Castle, in 1689, and asked permision for their countrymen to have a church of  their own, he flew into a passion and accused them of ingratitude and impertinence. "It is  evident," he wrote, " that they not only wa
2 CONC nt their own church, but their own magistrate and  their own prince." They pretended to have left France, said Simon bitterly, because of their  religious convictions, but in reality they wanted opportunities of leading a lazy and
2 CONC  indolent life ;  were people of the wrong stamp, he declared, entirely unacquainted with and unfit for the hard  life which was the lot of the farmer.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 199 “The present houses are charming, but the shelters of the first settlers were hastily built and  poor. They cut their way through the bushes, and chose indeed one superb site after another ; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 200  POMEGRANATES AT FRENCHHOEK.  DRAKENSTEIN AND FRENCHHOEK 
2 CONT
2 CONT it would have been difficult to do otherwise, but there was little time and money to spend on the adornment of the sheltering walls. Hence you will find near most of these graceful little houses the remains of an earlier ruder ere
2 CONC ction. Three of the finest houses are on sites granted to the three brothers de Villiers. Abraham de Villiers owned the farm of Laborie (commonly called L'Abri), surrounded by great oaks, with teak ceilings and floors to the dwell
2 CONC ing rooms, teak china cupboards let into the walls. Alas ! the thatch is gone, as it will soon go everywhere, on account of the heavy insurance asked for it ; and with the thatch down in most cases, for want of a little care and a
2 CONC  little knowledge of how to do things, comes the old gable. “
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham de Villiers (Viellers) van La Rochelle het in Mei 1689 na die Kaap gekom. Later in die jaar is hy met Suzanne Gardiol van La Costé getroud. In November 1689 was hy lid van die deputasie wat aansoek gedoen het om 'n aparte 
2 CONC kerkraad vir die Hugenote. Hy het een van die eerste diakens van die kerkraad van Drakenstein geword, verder heemraad en kaptein van die burgerkompanjie van Drakenstein. In 1711 is die plaas ‘Champagne’ in Olifantshoek aan hom toe
2 CONC geken. Hy het ook op ‘La Rochelle’, ‘Bosch-en-Dal’ en ‘Meerrust’ naby ‘Lekkerwyn’ geboer. In 1720 is hy op ‘Lekkerwyn’ oorlede. Vgl. Resolusies III, bl. 215, 247, 400, 446; C. Graham Botha, The French Refugees at the Cape, bl. 89,
2 CONC  121.
2 CONT
