0 @I6000000197218261457@ INDI
1 NAME Alexandre /Dagneau/
2 GIVN Alexandre
2 SURN Dagneau
2 _MARNM Dagneau de Douville
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1697
2 PLAC Sorel, Pierre-De Saurel, Quebec, Canada
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 NOV 1773
2 ADDR
3 CITY Montréal
3 STAE Province of Quebec
3 CTRY British Colony
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From an upcoming publication, information on the family:
2 CONT Three sons and three grandsons of Michel Dagneau de Douville (Officer in the Troupes de la Marine) and Marie Lamy all served as officers in the Troupes de la Marine during this time. A fourth son, Guillaume Dagneau de Douville et 
2 CONC de Lamothe, retired from the service in 1734, and a fifth son, Jean Dagneau de Douville had died in 1741. The family were well respected interpreters among the western tribes.
2 CONT 1. Alexandre Dagneau de Douville (1698-1773): 1716 cadet in Canada; 1716-1748 he participated in several western expeditions; 1730 married Marie Coulon de Villiers at Montréal; 1734 second ensign; 1741 ensign; 1739-1743 at fort Fr
2 CONC ontenac; 1750 lieutenant, commander at Sault Saint-Louis; 1751 at fort Saint-Frédéric; 1754 commander at fort de la Presqu’île; 1756 captain réformé; 1759 commander at fort Rouillé. He died at Montréal. 
2 CONT 2. Philippe Dagneau de La Saussaye (1700-1759): 1727 married Marie-Madeleine Raimbault at Montréal; 1734 second ensign in Canada. He died at fort Niagara.
2 CONT 3. Louis-Césaire Dagneau de Douville et de Quindré (1703-1766): 1724 cadet in Canada, at fort Saint-Joseph-des-Illinois; 1736 married Marie-Françoise-Anne Picoté de Belestre at Montréal; 1740-1752 at fort Saint-Joseph-des-Illinois
2 CONC ; 1756 at fort Duquesne; 1760 cadet à l’aiguillette; 1760-1763 at Détroit. He died at Détroit. 
2 CONT 1-S1. Alexandre-René Dagneau de Douville (1736-c. 1789):  son of Alexandre and Marie Coulon de Villiers. 1750 cadet in Canada; 1752 at fort Saint-Frédéric; 1753 sensig at the île du Vent; 1757 lieutenant; 1758 at la Grenade; 1759 
2 CONC lieutenant at Saint-Pierre. He died circa 1789 in Guadeloupe.
2 CONT 1-S2. Joseph Dagneau Douville (1737 - 1756): son of Alexandre and Marie Coulon de Villiers. On 23 March 1756 Enseigne en second Douville received orders from Capitaine Jean-Daniel Dumas, commanding at Fort Duquesne and la Belle Ri
2 CONC vière, to take 50 savages and scout the enemy movements below Fort Cumberland, Virginie, and to harass their convoys wherever possible and to burn their storehouses at Canagiechuic [the Conococheague] if practicable, with very spe
2 CONC cific instructions not to allow his savages to inflict cruelties on their captives. Ensign Douville was killed 7 April 1756 while attacking a small fort on the north branch of the Cacapon.  After his death and scalping by Virginie
2 CONC  soldiers, George Washington became involved, personally urging Governor Dinwiddie to pay his Virginie troops a bounty for Douville’s scalp, stating his hope that “although it is not an Indian’s, they will meet with an adequate re
2 CONC ward at least, as the monsieur’s is of much more consequence”. 
2 CONT 2-S1. Philippe-Thomas Dagneau de La Saussaye (1728-1757): son of Philippe and Marie-Madeleine Raimbault. 1747 cadet in Canada at fort Sainte-Thérèse; 1755 second ensign in Canada, married Marie-Anne Jarret de Verchères at Montréal
2 CONC ; 1757 at fort Duquesne. He died in an ambush at fort Duquesne, François Marie Picoté de Belestre was taken prisoner, Ensigns Dagneau, St.Ours and 3 soldiers were killed. “Dagneau, Dagneaux or d'Agneau brothers,” Trade Goods, acce
2 CONC ssed 23 February 2023, http://users.usinternet.com/dfnels/dagneau.htm.  Fournier, Officiers des troupes de la Marine, Kindle Edition, 317-319, #185-192. “Joseph Dagneau d'Ouville (1737 - 1756),” Geneaologie.quebec, accessed 23 Apr
2 CONC il 2023, https://genealogie.quebec/stemma4web/info/239274. Yves Drolet, Dictionnaire Généalogique de la Noblesse de la Nouvelle-France (Montréal: Bibliothèque et Archives Canada, 2015), 164. Accessed online: https://collections.ba
2 CONC nq.qc.ca/ark:/52327/bs2479072. “Enclosure: Jean-Daniel Dumas to Alexandre d’Agneau Douville, 23 March 1756,” National Archives Founders Online, National Archives, accessed 23 April, 2023, https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Wa
2 CONC shington/02-02-02-0332-0002. Henry J. Young, “A Note on Scalp Bounties in Pennsylvania,” Pennsylvania History: A Journal of Mid-Atlantic Studies 24, no. 3 (1957): 207–18, accessed 23 April, 2023. http://www.jstor.org/stable/277697
2 CONC 43.
