1 NAME Jacob /de Villiers/
2 GIVN Jacob
2 SURN de Villiers
2 _MARNM de Villiers
1 NAME Jacques //
2 GIVN Jacques
2 SURN de Villiers
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 29 APR 1703
2 ADDR
3 STAE Caep de Goede Hoop
3 CTRY South Africa
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 JUN 1734
2 ADDR
3 CITY Drakenstein
3 STAE Caep de Goede Hoop
3 CTRY South Africa
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Baptised Jacob Villiers  never called Jacobus or Jacques. 
2 CONT Jacob often a French name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Farmer at ''Nieuwe Dorp'' and ''De Goede Hoop'' in the Drakenstein district.  On 22 Dec 1722 he was appointed as a deacon of the Drakenstein parish of the Church, and in 1724 he married Louisa Joubert.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Extract from DP de Villiers, A History of the De Villiers Family, Nationale Boekhandel Bpk, 1960:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques de Villers' eldest son, Jean, died in infancy.  Jacobus, the second son, baptised on 14.09.1699, married Louise Joubert, widow of Jan Lombard.
2 CONT Above incorrect!! Jacobus baptised 1699  died before 1703
2 CONT Jacob de Villiers baptised in 1703 married Louise Joubert
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Nieuwedorp'' , a farm adjoining Goede Hoop, both Rhodes Fruit Farms at Groot Drakenstein today, was the home of Jacobus and Louise de Villiers.  He died in 1734 at the age of 35 years.  Why he died so young is not known. His wil
2 CONC l dated 20.01.1734 states that he was sick and in bed.  I have a photo of an early Cape Dutch house, ''Nieuwedorp''.  It appears to have been an interim house, H-shaped with simple gables but with thick walls.  The wall-cupboard f
2 CONC rom that house is now in the Lekkerwyn house, home of Mrs Pickstone.  It is a beautiful example of early wall cupboards.  This house has been demolished, and the Rhodes cottage is now standing approximately where this early de Vil
2 CONC liers home once stood.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacobus and Louise had a family of four children.  The eldest boy, Jacob, married Catherina Booysen, and is the ancestor of Brig JP de Villiers. Tielman Nieuwoudt de Villiers, first mayor of Pretoria, and Commissioner-General for 
2 CONC the Transvaal Republic in Paris in 1889, and his brother Paul de Villiers, Treasurer-General of the same Republic, and Senator Bruckner de Villiers, were descendants of this branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT The youngest son, Pieter, baptised on 23 June 1732 is that ancestor of mine who first owned ''La Provence'' in 1756.  He married three times and had eighteen children!
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.e-family.co.za/ffy/g5/p5832.htm First Fifty Years Project]<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Wife''' 	Louise Joubert b. b 6 Dec 1699, d. 1764<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''	
2 CONT * Margreta de Villiers+14 b. b 5 Jun 1725
2 CONT * Jacob de Villiers15 b. b 6 Apr 1727
2 CONT * Elizabeth de Villiers16 b. b 12 Mar 1730
2 CONT * Pieter de Villiers17 b. b 22 Jun 1732
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogiese/Genealogical #: b5
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT S A Geneaologies Volume 14 - suggest that there was a previous child Jacob who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT www.stamouers.com do not list this child.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Note: See the earlier Jacob's baptism entry on http://www.geni.com/people/Jacobus-de-Villiers/6000000018061796668
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism record: https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSV8-W94W-2?i=332
2 CONT
2 CONT NGK Drakenstein, Cape of Good Hope
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1703. 29 ditto [April]. Jacob. d' Vader Jacob Villier, d' Moeder Margariet Gardiol. Getuijgen Estienne Viret, en Elisabet Villiers.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT - Drakenstein Heemkring, compilers, Drakenstein I Baptisms., CD-ROM (Paarl) Drakenstein Heemkring, 2006, Baptism Register on http://www.e-family.co.za/ffy/index.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Inventaris van alle goederen en effecten als ab intestato sijn naargelaaten en met ’er dood ontruijmt door Margaretha Gardiol wed:w wijlen Jacob Villiers d’ oude ten behoeve van haar onder te noemene soo kinderen als kindskinderen
2 CONC , sijnde de meerderjaarige kinderen, gen:t
2 CONT
2 CONT * David
2 CONT * Abraham en
2 CONT * Jan Villiers, mitsgd:rs
2 CONT * Susanna Villiers getrouwt met den landbouwer Pieter Roux
2 CONT * Johanna Villiers gehuwt met den landbouwer Jurgen Jansz: Schoeman
2 CONT * Margaretha Villiers thans wed:w Hendrik Hoppe
2 CONT * Rachel Villiers getrouwt met den landbouwer Hendrik Moller, voorts
2 CONT * de naarvolgende kindskinderen, als Margaretha Villiers getrouwt met den landbouwer Pieter de Bruijn
2 CONT * Jacob
2 CONT * Pieter en
2 CONT * Elisabeth Villiers }deese drie thans nog minderjaarig '''bij representatie van wijlen hun vader Jacob Villiers''', en eijndelijk
2 CONT * Maria Marais gehuwt met den burger Petrus Johannes Bosman
2 CONT * Jacob bijde reets meerderjaarige, en
2 CONT * Susanna Marais
2 CONT * Elisabeth en
2 CONT * Margaretha Marias, thans nog minderjaarig bij plaatsvullinge van wijlen hun moeder Maria Elisabeth Villiers huijsvr: van den landbouwer Steeven Marais
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Aldus gedaan en g’inventariseert ter Weescamer aan Cabo de Goede Hoop den 21 Junij 1749.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Inventories of the Orphan Chamber Cape Town Archives Repository, South Africa Reference no.: MOOC8/8.16 on http://databases.tanap.net/mooc/]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Franschhoek, West Cape, South Africa noble de Villiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pieter Daniel de Villiers and Johanna Jacoba de Villiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT This lineage of Pieter de Villiers seem to be the clan of the De Villiers Family of the French Huguenots and Winemaking
2 CONT Three brothers –
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre de Villiers (1657-1720), Jacques de Villiers (1661-1735) and Abraham de Villiers (1659-1720) – arrived in the Cape Colony aboard the Zion in 1689.
2 CONT
2 CONT The French Huguenots farmed the wineries of these regions, this article below is a fantastic detailed history of these families.
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor van der Stel enthusiastically greeted the fleet and settled the families in and around Stellenbosch and the Drakenstein valley, with some in an area that quickly became known as Franschhoek (French Corner), and others in 
2 CONC nearby regions.[22] It was with the arrival of these refugees that wine making in the Cape Colony received the boost it needed.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Viticulture to Commemoration: French Huguenot Memory in the Cape Colony (1688-1824)
2 CONT
2 CONT LINK:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://quod.lib.umich.edu/w/wsfh/0642292.0047.007?view=text;rgn=main
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT The Journal of the Western Society for French History
2 CONT
2 CONT show less
