1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Huwelik/Marriage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:S3HT-6LX3-JMF?i=13&cc=1478678&personaUrl=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AVFTM-R4M
2 CONT
2 CONT Doopregister NGK Drakenstein 2 Okt 1707:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSV8-W944-1?i=336
2 CONT
2 CONT Image 337 page 2
2 CONT
2 CONT getuygen Abraham Villiers en Susanna Taillefer
2 CONT
2 CONT ..........................................................................................
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogiese/Genealogical #: b8
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham farmed on De Goede Hoop and Rhenosterrug.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham farmed the following properties:-	
2 CONT
2 CONT De Goode Hoop and Bossendal in Drakenstein	
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhenosterrug and De Leeuwendans in Wellington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married to Susanna Joubert, 29 May 1735 in Paarl
2 CONT
2 CONT Married to Johanna Lombard, 3 November 1737 in Stellenbosch
2 CONT
2 CONT Son Jan de Villiers born 24 October 1745 in Drakenstein
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Married to Susanna Joubert, 29 May 1735 in Paarl
2 CONT
2 CONT Married to Johanna Lombard, 3 November 1737 in Stellenbosch
2 CONT
2 CONT Son Jan de Villiers born 24 October 1745 in Drakenstein
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham was born at Drakenstein on 2 Oct 1707 and he died there on 5 Jun 1763. He farmed De Goede Hoop, in Drakenstein, and Rhenosterrug and De Leeuwendaas in Wellington. He married Susanna Joubert at Paarl on 29 May 1735. She was
2 CONC  the daughter of Pieter Joubert and Susanna de Villiers and was born in 1719 but died in 1736 when she was only 17 years old. She left a daughter Susanna. The next year , 1737, Abraham married Johanna Lombard who had been baptised
2 CONC  on 2 Feb 1721, the daughter of Jan Lombard and Louisa Joubert. She died in 1772 having given birth to 11 children. Her 5th child, and Abraham’s 6th, was Jan de Villiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Bossendal later called Boschendal
2 CONT
2 CONT            http://www.boschendal.com/heritage.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Boschendal is one of the oldest wine producers in the New World with a French viticultural heritage dating back to 1685. The farm "Bossendal" (wood and dale) was granted in 1685 to French émigré Jean le Long by the governor of the
2 CONC  Cape, Simon van der Stel. Le Long was one of the many Protestant Huguenots who fled from France to escape religious persecution after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1715 the property, an immensely prestigious 3,500 hectares, stretching from Simonsberg to the Groot Drakenstein mountains, was purchased by the De Villiers brothers, Pierre, Abraham and Jacques, who were also French Huguenot vi
2 CONC gnerons and under their ownership Boschendal prospered and grew. In 1812, Paul de Villiers, grandson of Jacques, built the impressive Boschendal Manor House, now a national monument. The farm remained in the De Villiers family for
2 CONC  164 years until 1879. It was then sold to a Dutch farmer, D J Retief, who went insolvent seven years later and the property was purchased by J G C Myburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT The property was later purchased by Cecil Rhodes.
2 CONT --------------------
