2 CONC dfc8d93b4751 MyHeritage Family Trees] via husband [http://www.geni.com/profile-100255076 Pierre Charles Boucher] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 22 2014, 19:29:19 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Born from marriage in 1807. Endured the 1816 Battle of Seven Oaks in. The first flag flown by the Metis was in 1815, the Infinity Flag. Between 1820 and 1850, Métis self-identity further crystallizes as hundreds of Red River Métis
2 CONC  and Scots/Orkney “Halfbreeds” begin working as free traders, fur trade boatmen, bison hunters and farmers. During this time, the Métis call themselves “gens libres,” “Otipemisiwak” or the “Free People” because of their desire to 
2 CONC be free. They are also known as “Apeetogosan,” “Wissakodewinimi,”“chicot,” and “Bois-brûlés” because of their mixed heritage. The two groups begin to intermarry and begin to see that they have common interests against Hudson’s Bay
2 CONC  Company trading monopoly and governance. In 1869 the Métis of Red River establish the Métis National Committee and formed a provisional government. Also one of the oldest anthems known. Between August 1870 and March 1873, the “Re
2 CONC ign of Terror” is conducted against the Métis by the Red River Expeditionary Force under the command of Garnet Wolseley. The force is sent west by the federal government to “pacify” the new province. Métis men are murdered and Mét
2 CONC is women and girls are raped by the Canadian soldiers. In addition, many settlers begin to arrive from Ontario and are openly hostile to the Métis. Louis Riel, fearing for his life, flees to the United States. The appalling social
2 CONC  climate in the new province forces the majority of the Métis to disperse to the South Branch Métis settlements around Batoche throughout the 1870s. Louis Riel is elected three times to represent a Manitoba riding — once in a gene
2 CONC ral election and twice in two by-elections held in October 1873, February 1874, and September 1874. He never takes his seat, fearing for his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Post-1885 Facing racism and marginalization, the Métis become the Forgotten People. Dislocated from their traditional lands, many Métis moved to other parts of Canada and the United States in search of work. Many more remained on 
2 CONC or near their traditional lands attempting to protect what they could of their way of life.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.canadiangeographic.com/educational_products/activities/ipac_gfm/Using_the_timeline_Metis_nation_history_EN_LR.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT In his speech to the assembly, North Bay Mayor Al McDonald proved he was open for conversations by announcing his cell phone number.
2 CONT
2 CONT The North Bay Métis Council has been active since 1995. It was the first council to establish an office and provide programs for citizens. Métis senators provide an elder’s presence in keeping the culture alive. Local Senator Marl
2 CONC ene Greenwood is an ancestor of the founder of Fort La Ronde on LaVase Island.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The North West Co. trading post was built by Denys de La Ronde. It was closed in 1821 and moved to Garden Island on the Sturgeon River following the merger of the North West and the Hudson's Bay Companies. The fort reportedly burn
2 CONC ed down in 1821.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greenwood explained La Ronde was one of the first names in the Métis community. “Eustacian and Tuscon were brothers that looked after that fort.  The La Ronde name was a title given from the French king to our original ancestor De
2 CONC nys. One of the Denys took a bullet for the king and saved the kings life. Land was given and the title La Ronde.” https://www.baytoday.ca/local-news/metis-nation-of-ontario-sets-sights-on-self-governance-376229
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT (Research):RECORD OF INTERMENTS 1888, PAGE 12 date death 15 July 1888 date burial 17 July 1888 name Boucher, Julie (nee Laronde) age 85 years priest Rev. J. Michel place death Penetanguishene
