1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pauline_F%C3%A9licit%C3%A9_de_Mailly English - '''Wikipedia ....''']
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Early life and family====
2 CONT Pauline Félicité was born the second daughter of Louis de Mailly, marquis de Nesle et de Mailly, Prince d'Orange (1689 - 1767), and his wife, Armande Félice de La Porte Mazarin (1691 - 1729). Her parents had been married in 1709. 
2 CONC Her mother was the daughter of Paul Jules de La Porte, duc Mazarin et de La Meilleraye (1666 - 1731), the son of the famous adventuress, Hortense Mancini, the niece of Cardinal Mazarin. Pauline Félicité had four full sisters:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Louise Julie de Mailly, Mademoiselle de Mailly, comtesse de Mailly (1710 - 1751),
2 CONT # Diane Adélaïde de Mailly, Mademoiselle de Montcavrel, duchesse de Lauraguais (1714 - 1769),
2 CONT # Hortense Félicité de Mailly, Mademoiselle de Chalon, marquise de Flavacourt (1715 - 1763). 
2 CONT # Marie Anne de Mailly, Mademoiselle de Monchy, marquise de La Tournelle, duchesse de Châteauroux (1717–1744).
2 CONT
2 CONT The only one of the de Nesle sisters not to become one of Louis XV's mistresses was the Hortense Félicité. Louise Julie was the first sister to attract the king followed by Pauline Félicité, but it was Marie Anne who was the most 
2 CONC successful in manipulating him and becoming politically powerful.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pauline Félicité also had a younger half-sister, Henriette de Bourbon (1725 - 1780), Mademoiselle de Verneuil, from her mother's relationship with the duc de Bourbon, the chief minister of Louis XV from 1723 to 1726.
2 CONT
2 CONT In her youth, Pauline Félicité was known as Mademoiselle de Nesle.
2 CONT ====Mistress to Louis XV====
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1738, Pauline wrote to her elder sister, Madame de Mailly, the king's official mistress, asking to be invited to court. She received the invitation, and during her stay proceeded to seduce the king, who fell passionately in lov
2 CONC e with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mademoiselle de Nesle then became the second official mistress of Louis XV, although her sister kept the position of maîtresse en titre. The king lavished her with gifts, the greatest being the castle of Choisy-le-Roi, newly decor
2 CONC ated in blue and silver. To provide her an appropriate status at court, the king arranged for her to marry a nobleman only too pleased to leave the couple alone. On 28 September 1739, Mademoiselle de Nesle married Jean Baptiste Fé
2 CONC lix Hubert de Vintimille, marquis de Vintimille, comte du Luc (b. 1720), who departed to the country after their wedding. The new marquise de Ventimille soon became pregnant by the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Madame de Vintimille was described as graceful with a long, swan-like neck, but not beautiful. She was much more ambitious than her older sister and predecessor, Madame de Mailly, and possessed a great desire for money and politic
2 CONC al influence; her arrogance quickly made her hated within the court and by the people.
2 CONT
2 CONT She could have been as politically influential as her two successors, Madame de Châteauroux and Madame de Pompadour, if not more, but her period as royal mistress was cut short; she died while giving birth to the son of the king i
2 CONC n 1741.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her corpse was placed at Lit-de-parade in the town of Versailles, but during the night the guards left the room to drink and a mob broke in and mutilated the corpse of "the king's whore".
2 CONT
2 CONT Both the king and her older sister, Madame de Mailly, were deeply devastated by the death of their lover and sister, and Madame de Mailly is said to have begun to wash the feet of the poor as a Catholic sign of remorse.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of the king and Madame de Ventimille was named Louis after his father and given the title of duc de Luc. He so resembled his father that he was called Demi-Louis, "small Louis". He was raised by his aunt, Madame de Mailly.
2 CONC  The king took care of his needs but never paid him much attention. Later, Madame de Pompadour wanted to marry her daughter to him, but the king would not allow it.
2 CONT ====Sources====
2 CONT This page is a translation of its French equivalent.
2 CONT
