1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Maunsell White, Jr., attended the University of Virginia from 1849-1851. He married Elizabeth (Bettie) Porter Bradford, a niece of Jefferson Davis. He lived in New Orleans and at his plantation in Plaquemines Paris
2 CONC h known as Junior Place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Times-Picayune, New Orleans, March 4, 1896, Page 7
2 CONT
2 CONT "OBSEQUIES OF MAUNSELL WHITE
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Yesterday the remains of Maunsell White, Jr., were laid to rest in the family tomb at the firemen's Cemetery, in this city, after the celebration of the requiem high mass at the Jesuits' church, on Baronne street, by Fathers Sempl
2 CONC e and Downey. The ceremony was attended by a large concourse of friends of the deceased and of his family connections. The pallbearers were: Dr. J. B. Wilkinson, Henry Deuis, James Miltenberger, Hon. Theo. S. Wilkinson, John Barkl
2 CONC ey and James White.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maunsell White, Jr., was the only son of the late Colonel Maunsell White, who was a captain under Jackson at the battle of New Orleans, and a colonel under Taylor in the Mexican war. He was born in this city in his father's house,
2 CONC  which still stands at the corner of St. Charles and Julia streets. He entered West Point by appointment from President Taylor, but a physical infirmity, deafness, coming upon him, he was debarred from continuing his course at tha
2 CONC t institution. He settled in Plaquemines parish, La., and engaged in planting until the disasters which came upon sugar cultivation in this state, causing him to abandon all connection with that industry. He moved to Kentucky and 
2 CONC embarked in farming, which occupation he pursued until the period of his fatal illness. He married Miss Elizabeth Bradford, a niece of Joseph and Jefferson Davis, and this union was a most felicitous one. His eldest son, Maunsell,
2 CONC  occupies a very prominent and responsible position in the metallurgic department of the Bethlehem Iron Works in Pennsylvania; his son Carl, who married the daughter of Robert Mitchell, of Cincinnati, is engaged in business in Ken
2 CONC tucky, Sidney who married Miss Tobin, has recently engaged in enterprises in this city. His eldest daughter married R. C. P. Wilkinson, and the second married Mr. A. Ringgold Brousseau, both of this city.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two younger daughters have been the recipients of many attentions and have made many friends during their recent visit to relatives here.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Maunsell White was a man of exceptional character. In the many adversities he was called upon to face he rose superior to every trial, and entered upon every endeavor with intelligent and patient energies. When measured by the
2 CONC  high standard of the performance of duty in every particular, as a man, a husband and a father, his life was preeminently a success." 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
