1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Maria Elisabet de Villiers [1] might have been born on the 27th of December 1761 [1][3][4][2], certain is that she was baptized on the 30th May 1762. [7] in Drakenstein in the then Dutch Cape Colony [7] (Cape [Sout
2 CONC h Africa]). [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 9 Jul 1780. [46] On the 9th of July 1780 Maria married Johannes Hendricús Redelijkhúijsen. [12][47] Their only child Johannes Hermanus Redelinghuys was baptized on the 2nd of December 1781. He died in the year 1783. [21][
2 CONC 48] In 1785 Johannes sold his house in Cape Town and left with his wife Maria Elisabeth de Villiers for the Netherlands where he sought contact with influential patriots and worked on a comprehensive petition. [1] In the Netherlan
2 CONC ds he published a pamphlet on the perceived treachery of one of his co-representatives, Marthinus Adrianus Bergh, [49] great-great-grandson of the infamous Olof Bergh. Maria died in October 1793 in the Netherlands and she was buri
2 CONC ed 17 October 1793, in the cemetery of the Westerkerk, Amsterdam. [1][50] Johannes Redelinghuis later became editor of the Nationale Courante and from 1801 he was a clerk of the Bureau ter Conversie der Staatsschulden in the Hague
2 CONC . A year later he died of tuberculosis. [1] His [second] wife Maria Marozee and daughters received a free passage to the Cape and in 1806 were in possession of his two farms on the Sonderend River. [1] Sources
2 CONT
