1 OCCU Owner of Honswyk (1772 to 1784) in Paarl and in Stellenbosch he owned Vredenburg & Oude Libertas (from 1784) including MonRepos/Onderpapagaaiberg from 1813. Member of the District School Commission and Elder of the DR Church in St
2 CONC ellenbosch.  
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QPS1-1M6W Probate Record]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jacobus Petrus ROUX''' was born on 7 Aug 1750 in Drakenstein, Cape, South Africa. '''Born on farm Driefontein'''. He owned the '''farm Honswyk (Under Oaks, Estherdal, Irene, and St. John collectively known as Nantes) from 1772 
2 CONC to 1784''' in Paarl where he resided until he acquired '''Vredenburg''', Oude Libertas and Throughend (Klein Vredenburg) in Stellenbosch in 1784. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He built the historic dwelling on Vredenburg in 1789 where he resided until his death in 1831.
2 CONT
2 CONT He acquired Mon Repos (Onderpapagaaiberg) in 1813 and sold it to Abraham Albertus de Villiers (Pieter Daniel / sn) in 1819. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm Estherdal, formerly Hon(d)swij(c)k [Hondswyk] and now known (since 2004) as Under Oaks – situated at Dal Josaphat, Drakenstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''UPPER VREDENBURG''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT This farm was granted in 1692 to the Heemraad of Stellenbosch, Gerhard van der Bijl, it was over 52 morgen in size. Van der Bijl had been settled there since 1680. After his death, in 1722, his son Pieter van der Bijl became owner
2 CONC  and during his ownership and that of his widow the farm was enlarged by the addition of land bought from neighbours. When in 1744 it became the property of their son, Gerrit van der Bijl, it was over 79 morgen in size ; his widow
2 CONC , Elisabeth Grové, in turn added part of Oude Libertas and increased the size to 91 morgen. She remarried in 1773; her second husband was Diederik Jacob Bleumer; this marriage soon ended in divorce. In 1784, after Bleumer's death,
2 CONC  the property was acquired by '''Jacobus Roux''', a brother of Paul Roux, the builder of Lower Vredenburg, and it remained in the hands of his various descendants until 1897, when it became a Rhodes Fruit Farm. It is now an agricu
2 CONC ltural research farm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT VREDENBURG * ( -Vlottenburg ) also Lower Vredenburg , now Vredenheim . This farm ( which De Bosdari erroneously calls Digteby ) was granted in 1691 to Hendrik Elbertsz ( not the man mentioned by De Bosdari , but his son ; the fath
2 CONC er's farm was By den Weg , across the road ) . It was over 62 morgen in size . In 1716 it was acquired , together with the adjoining farm Vlottenburg ( which had been granted in 1707 to two Huguenots, Pierre Rochefort and Gérard H
2 CONC anseret , in partnership ) by Johannes Wessels . In 1742 they passed from his widow to Johannes Loubser ; thence , via several intermediate owners , to Paul Roux sr . Lower Vredenburg was transferred from the estate of Paul Roux t
2 CONC o his second son , '''Jacob Roux''' , who in 1789 built the historic dwelling on Vredenburg..
2 CONT
2 CONT '''MON REPOS''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT This little property was a freehold grant of just under six morgen, made in 1785 to Willem Wium. The grant says it was ''annex zijn plaats'', and his old farm was Oude Libertas; it is therefore unlikely that Wium built a house on 
2 CONC Mon Repos. The next transfer is dated 1813. In that year both farms were acquired by '''Jacobus Petrus Roux''' who already owned Upper Vredenburg, and in 1819 they went to Abraham Albertus de Villiers (Pieter Daniel / sn - see La 
2 CONC Dauphine), who in 1825 sold Mon Repos (then called Onder Papagaaisberg) to David Johannes de Villiers ... 
2 CONT
2 CONT KLEIN VREDENBURG
2 CONT
2 CONT Nearby lies Klein Vredenburg, a small farm which was granted in 1691 to Hendrick Elbertz, Heemraad of Stellenbosch. Apparently he throve, as all the farmers did during the van der Stel days, for thirteen years later he owned the f
2 CONC ine farm of Aan den Weg in Stellenbosch Kloof. His sons, Johannes and Nicolaas, were among the nine mutineers against the Government in 1706 who refused to appear at the Castle to answer the charge as recorded in the Archives of '
2 CONC Writing, drafting, and signing of libels and defamatory letters, full of treason and insult, falsely forged against the authorities here '. Chief amongst these letters was the charge against the Governor, Secunde and Minister, dra
2 CONC wn up by Adam Tas at the instigation of Huysing and others.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''TROUGHEND''' (formerly Klein Vredenburg)
2 CONT
2 CONT This farm is a deduction from Upper Vredenburg, made in 1820 by '''JP Roux''' in favour of Hendrik Vos Hendrik / sn; it was 11 morgen in extent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference: A Guide to the Old Buildings of the Cape. A Survey of Extant Architecture from Before C1910 in the Area of Cape Town-Calvinia-Colesberg-Uitenhage
2 CONT By Mary Alexander Cook · 2004
2 CONT
2 CONT Granted in 1692 to Gerhard Van Der Bijl. Under Van Der Bijl ownership through 3 generations the farm was expanded to include current Throughend and parts of Oude Libertas. In 1784 the farm was acquired by '''Jacobus Roux''' and re
2 CONC mained in their family for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT Vredenburg , its vineyards flanking the tawny waters of the Eerste River , stands proudly under a splendid late holbol gable that , for sheer beauty , can vie with those of Ida's Valley , Morgenster and Hazendal . the by Uiterwyk 
2 CONC At the head of Stellenbosch Kloof stands. Designed on the generous H - plan , Vredenburg's spaciousness and elaborate front gable reflect its original owner's prosperity The simple lines of this holbol gable at Vredenburg contrast
2 CONC  with the gable on the front façade . Uiterwyk's fine baroque gable is dated 1791. Neethlingshof's central neoclassical gable is dated 1814 , although the. beauty of the façade . Fortunately , the wonderful gable remained unscathe
2 CONC d
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1691, the property on which Vredenburg stands was granted to Hendrik Elbertsz , one of the Cape's first free burghers . It was not until 1789 , however , that a thatched and gabled house was built at Vreden- burg - the same hou
2 CONC se that can be seen ... adjacent farm , Vlottenburg . An early owner was Jan de Villiers , who was also master of the nearby farm De Wolwedans , now Neethlingshof . On the night of 25 July 1761 , De Villiers and his young wife , A
2 CONC nna Aletta Hugo , were murdered at Vredenburg by a slave , leaving four children under the age of eight . Later the property was acquired by Paul Roux , a direct descendant of the Paul Roux who had arrived at the Cape with the Hug
2 CONC uenots in 1688. On the death of Paul Roux of Vredenburg in 1830, Vlottenburg was inherited by his eldest son , another Paul , while Vredenburg went 
2 CONT
2 CONT .... can be seen there today , although much altered . In the intervening hundred years , Vredenburg changed hands several times , at one stage being enlarged by
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference: Cape Dutch houses and other old favourites By Phillida Brooke Simons · 2000
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT Daar bestaan ’n foto van ’n ou nederige T-vormige woonhuis op Vredenburg wat in 1965 geneem is. Daar bestaan ook ’n foto van die tweede woonhuis wat deur '''Jacob Roux''' in 1789 gebou is. Net agter diè huis kan ’n mens die eerste
2 CONC  woning sien. Hierdie foto is sowat 100 jaar gelede geneem. Dit kan met redelike sekerheid aanvaar word dat dit foto’s is van die oorspronklike woonhuis van Vredenburg wat voor 1686 gebou is, want die vroegste gedateerde afbeeldin
2 CONC g van ’n Stellenbosch woning (1686) verskyn op die grondbrief van die plaas Vredenburg, uit die pen van Johannes Mulder, Stellenbosch se eerste landdros en landmeter. Oorspronklik was dit ’n een-vleuel drie-vertrek huis (kombuis, 
2 CONC voorhuis en slaapkamer - elk het die volle breedte van die vleuel beslaan). Later is hierdie huis vergroot in ’n T-vormige huis. In ou Kaapse huise ongeag van wat sy grondplan was, is die kombuis altyd aan die end van ’n vleuel ge
2 CONC plaas en aan die stertpunt van die T as dit ’n T-vormige huis was, want die gewel is gebruik om die skoorsteen van die vuurherd te stut. Omdat die kombuis van hierdie huis nie aan die stertpunt van die T geplaas was nie moes die s
2 CONC tertpunt dus later aangebou gewees het.
2 CONT
2 CONT Na Johannes dood het sy jong vroutjie op 1-4-1719 weer getrou met die wewenaar Johannes (Jan) Cloete. Jan het drie klein kindertjies gehad ( twee seuns en ’n dogter) toe hy met Geertruy getroud is. Geen kinders is uit die huwelik 
2 CONC van Jan en Geertruy gebore nie. So het Vredenburg en Vlottenburg gekom in die hande van die Cloetes. Op 30-11-1736 was die plaas nog in besit van Geertruy Wessels (geb. Pretorius), weduwee van Jan Cloete. Op 9-11-1736 verkoop wedu
2 CONC wee Cloete die helfte van Vlottenburg/Vredenburg aan Hester Ternwinkel, wed. van Pieter v.d. Bijl. Op 30-11-1736 koop wed Cloete weer ’n gedeelte van wed. P. v.d. Bijl - dit lyk of dit slegs ’n ruiling was. Op 21-2-1742 koop Johan
2 CONC nes Lobster Vlottenburg van wed. Cloete, en op 24-7-1743 koop Mattheus Guillamet weer Vlottenburg. Op 26-5-1756 koop Jan de Villiers, P/seun, Vlottenburg. Ander grondeienaars van persele te Vlottenburg/Vredenburg was Paul Roux, He
2 CONC ndrik Vos, Pieter Gerhard Myburgh, Francois Roos, Cecil John Rhodes, Piere Rockefort, G. Hanseret en Antonie Vlotman.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
