1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Daniel was a member of Detroit’s first Board of Education, which explains the piles of books.
2 CONT
2 CONT CAMPAU, DANIEL JOSEPH, SR. b. 11-18-1813 d. 2-14-1883 Bur. Sec. E, Lots 50 & 59. Son of Joseph Campau. At age l9, he left home and his job working in his father's trading posts. With money he saved as a clerk, he bought his own dr
2 CONC y-goods store in 1834. In 1842, he was forced into bankruptcy by the failure of the old Michigan Bank. He started all over, working as a clerk and investing extra money in real estate. Eventually he became a wealthy real estate ag
2 CONC ent. He was a selfmade man, having received no financial assistance from Joseph Campau, his famous father and the wealthiest man in Michigan, as he struggled to make it on his own. In 1842, he became a member of the first Board of
2 CONC  Education of Detroit, and the Board's Inspector of Schools. He also served as the Board's treasurer for six terms. He was treasurer of the City of Detroit, 1842-1844, and Wayne County treasurer, 1845-1850. He married Mary Frances
2 CONC  Palms. They had three children: Daniel Joseph, Jr., (who at age 72 married Katherine DeMille, and who is presently buried in Elmwood Cemetery); Louis Palms Campau; and Mrs. Adelaide Thompson, wife of former Detroit Mayor William 
2 CONC G. Thompson.
2 CONT
2 CONT He suffered the last 25 years of his life with paralysis.  He had to be carried in and out of the carriage.  He couldn't take a step.  He was in a lot of pain and must have welcomed death.  Before that he was so alive and had many
2 CONC  friends.  
